  const endMarker = `<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ proxychains msfconsole`;

  const zhSection = `<p><strong>Port Forwarding（端口转发）</strong> 是把访问某个主机/端口的流量，转交给另一个主机/端口。它常见于两类场景：一类是把目标主机上“只对本地开放”的服务映射到我们自己的机器；另一类是借助已经拿下的主机，把访问能力继续延伸到它所连接的内网。</p>
<h2>选择 Pivot 点并建立 Tunneling</h2>
<p>这一部分可以拆成两个典型场景：</p>
<ul>
<li>如果你已经知道想访问的服务端口，比如目标机器本地监听的 MySQL，那么优先使用 <code>SSH 本地端口转发</code>。</li>
<li>如果你还不知道目标内网另一侧有哪些主机和服务，那么更适合先建立 <code>SOCKS</code> 隧道，再配合 <code>proxychains</code> 做枚举和横向访问。</li>
</ul>
<h3>SSH 本地端口转发</h3>
<img alt="Pasted image 20250529214631" src="assets/posts/pivoting-tunneling-port-forwarding/Pasted image 20250529214631.png"/>
<p>先看这个场景：攻击机位于 <code>10.10.15.x</code>，已经能够通过 SSH 登录 Ubuntu 跳板机 <code>10.129.202.64</code>；但 MySQL 只监听在跳板机本地的 <code>3306</code>，外部无法直接访问。目标就是把远端的 <code>3306</code> 映射到我们本机的一个端口上。</p>
<p><strong>1. 先确认目标主机暴露了哪些端口</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ nmap -sT -p22,3306 10.129.202.64

Starting Nmap 7.92 ( https://nmap.org ) at 2022-02-24 12:12 EST
Nmap scan report for 10.129.202.64
Host is up (0.12s latency).

PORT     STATE  SERVICE
22/tcp   open   ssh
3306/tcp closed mysql
Nmap done: 1 IP address (1 host up) scanned in 0.68 seconds</code></pre>
<p>这里最重要的信息不是“3306 关闭”本身，而是它说明了：MySQL 并不是完全不存在，而是没有对外开放。这种情况下，本地端口转发正合适。</p>
<p><strong>2. 建立本地端口转发</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ssh -L 1234:localhost:3306 ubuntu@10.129.202.64</code></pre>
<p><code>-L 1234:localhost:3306</code> 的含义是：让本机监听 <code>1234</code> 端口，然后把所有打到这个端口的流量，通过 SSH 会话转发给远端主机上的 <code>localhost:3306</code>。</p>
<p><strong>3. 验证转发是否生效</strong></p>
<p>首先确认本机是否真的开始监听 <code>1234</code>：</p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ netstat -antp | grep 1234

tcp        0      0 127.0.0.1:1234          0.0.0.0:*               LISTEN      4034/ssh
tcp6       0      0 ::1:1234                :::*                    LISTEN      4034/ssh</code></pre>
<p>上面的输出说明监听已经建立，而且负责监听的进程就是 <code>ssh</code>。</p>
<p>然后再对本地端口做一次服务识别：</p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ nmap -v -sV -p1234 localhost

PORT     STATE SERVICE VERSION
1234/tcp open  mysql   MySQL 8.0.28-0ubuntu0.20.04.3</code></pre>
<p>当本地的 <code>1234</code> 被识别成 MySQL 时，就意味着我们已经可以把它当成“本机上的数据库端口”来使用了。</p>
<p><strong>4. 一次转发多个端口</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ssh -L 1234:localhost:3306 -L 8080:localhost:80 ubuntu@10.129.202.64</code></pre>
<p>如果目标上还有别的只对本地开放的服务，比如 Web 管理页面，也可以在同一条 SSH 命令里继续追加多个 <code>-L</code> 参数。</p>
<h3>基于 SSH 和 SOCKS 的动态端口转发</h3>
<p>另一种更典型的 pivot 场景是：你已经拿到了 Ubuntu 跳板机，但还不知道它连接的内网另一侧到底有哪些服务。这时没法像上一个例子那样只转发单个端口，而是应该先建立一个 SOCKS 代理，把后续探测流量都绕到跳板机后面去。</p>
<p><strong>1. 先确认跳板机是否真的连着另一张内网</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">ubuntu@WEB01:~$ ifconfig

ens192: inet 10.129.202.64
ens224: inet 172.16.5.129
lo:     inet 127.0.0.1</code></pre>
<p>这里最关键的是看到两张网卡：</p>
<ul>
<li><code>ens192</code> 在 <code>10.129.202.0/16</code> 这一侧，攻击机能够到达它。</li>
<li><code>ens224</code> 在 <code>172.16.5.0/23</code> 这一侧，而这正是攻击机原本无法直接访问的内网。</li>
</ul>
<p>这就说明它具备充当 <code>pivot host</code> 的条件。</p>
<img alt="Pasted image 20250529220839" src="assets/posts/pivoting-tunneling-port-forwarding/Pasted image 20250529220839.png"/>
<p><strong>2. 在本地启动 SSH 动态端口转发</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ssh -D 9050 ubuntu@10.129.202.64</code></pre>
<p><code>-D 9050</code> 会在你的本地机器上创建一个 SOCKS 监听器。之后所有发往 <code>127.0.0.1:9050</code> 的 TCP 流量，都会先进入 SSH 隧道，再从 Ubuntu 跳板机转发到它可达的网络。</p>
<p><strong>3. 告诉 proxychains 走这个 SOCKS 代理</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ tail -4 /etc/proxychains.conf

# meanwile
# defaults set to "tor"
socks4 127.0.0.1 9050</code></pre>
<p>配置完成后，凡是通过 <code>proxychains</code> 启动的 TCP 工具，都会把流量先送到本地 <code>9050</code>，再经由 SSH 隧道转发出去。</p>
<p><strong>4. 先做小范围探测，再锁定具体主机</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ proxychains nmap -v -sn 172.16.5.1-200

ProxyChains-3.1 (http://proxychains.sf.net)
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.5:80-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-OK
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.6:80-&lt;--timeout</code></pre>
<p>这一步的目标不是拿到完整端口画像，而是确认“这条隧道确实能把探测包送进内网”。不过在真实环境里，整网段扫一遍往往很慢，所以更实用的做法通常是先缩小范围，或者直接扫描已知主机。</p>
<p><strong>5. 通过 SOCKS 隧道对目标主机做 TCP 全连接扫描</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ proxychains nmap -v -Pn -sT 172.16.5.19

ProxyChains-3.1 (http://proxychains.sf.net)
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.19:445-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-OK
Discovered open port 445/tcp on 172.16.5.19
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.19:135-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-OK
Discovered open port 135/tcp on 172.16.5.19
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.19:3389-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-OK
Discovered open port 3389/tcp on 172.16.5.19</code></pre>
<p>这里要特别注意两点：</p>
<ul>
<li>通过 <code>proxychains</code> 跑 Nmap 时，优先用 <code>-sT</code> 这种 TCP 全连接扫描，不要用半连接 SYN 扫描。</li>
<li>很多 Windows 主机默认不会回 ICMP，因此对已知主机直接加上 <code>-Pn</code> 会更稳。</li>
</ul>
<p>现在我们已经知道 <code>172.16.5.19</code> 开了 <code>445</code>、<code>135</code> 和 <code>3389</code>。这就意味着隧道已经不仅能“通”，而且已经足够支撑后续的 RDP 登录、Metasploit 辅助枚举或其它横向动作。</p>
<p><strong>通过 proxychains 使用 xfreerdp</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">proxychains xfreerdp /v:172.16.5.19 /u:victor /p:pass@123</code></pre>
<p>如果凭据有效，这条命令就能把 RDP 流量也一起送进 SOCKS 隧道。也就是说，从这一步开始，后面的 Metasploit、RDP、SMB 等动作，本质上都已经是在“借跳板机进内网”了。</p>
<h3>使用 Metasploit 与 Proxychains 结合使用</h3>
`;

  const enSection = `<p><strong>Port Forwarding</strong> sends traffic received on one host and port to a different host and port. In practice, we usually use it in two situations: to reach services bound only to a pivot host's localhost, or to extend our reach into an internal network that the pivot host can access but we cannot.</p>
<h2>Choosing a Pivot Point and Building the Tunnel</h2>
<p>This section is easier to follow if you split it into two common cases:</p>
<ul>
<li>If you already know the exact service you want, such as a MySQL instance bound to localhost on the pivot host, use <code>SSH local port forwarding</code>.</li>
<li>If you do not yet know which internal hosts and ports matter, build a <code>SOCKS</code> tunnel first and then enumerate through <code>proxychains</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h3>SSH local port forwarding</h3>
<img alt="Pasted image 20250529214631" src="assets/posts/pivoting-tunneling-port-forwarding/Pasted image 20250529214631.png"/>
<p>In this scenario, the attack host can SSH into the Ubuntu pivot host at <code>10.129.202.64</code>, but MySQL listens only on the pivot host's local <code>3306</code>. The goal is to expose that remote service on a local port we can use directly.</p>
<p><strong>1. Confirm the visible services first</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ nmap -sT -p22,3306 10.129.202.64

Starting Nmap 7.92 ( https://nmap.org ) at 2022-02-24 12:12 EST
Nmap scan report for 10.129.202.64
Host is up (0.12s latency).

PORT     STATE  SERVICE
22/tcp   open   ssh
3306/tcp closed mysql
Nmap done: 1 IP address (1 host up) scanned in 0.68 seconds</code></pre>
<p>The important point is not just that <code>3306</code> looks closed from the outside. It tells us MySQL is not exposed publicly, which makes it a strong candidate for local port forwarding.</p>
<p><strong>2. Create the local forwarding rule</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ssh -L 1234:localhost:3306 ubuntu@10.129.202.64</code></pre>
<p><code>-L 1234:localhost:3306</code> means: listen on local port <code>1234</code>, then forward anything received there through the SSH session to the pivot host's <code>localhost:3306</code>.</p>
<p><strong>3. Verify that the tunnel is active</strong></p>
<p>First, confirm that your local machine is actually listening on port <code>1234</code>:</p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ netstat -antp | grep 1234

tcp        0      0 127.0.0.1:1234          0.0.0.0:*               LISTEN      4034/ssh
tcp6       0      0 ::1:1234                :::*                    LISTEN      4034/ssh</code></pre>
<p>The listener belongs to <code>ssh</code>, which confirms the forwarding socket is in place.</p>
<p>Then fingerprint the forwarded local port itself:</p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ nmap -v -sV -p1234 localhost

PORT     STATE SERVICE VERSION
1234/tcp open  mysql   MySQL 8.0.28-0ubuntu0.20.04.3</code></pre>
<p>Once local port <code>1234</code> is identified as MySQL, we have effectively turned the remote database into a locally reachable service.</p>
<p><strong>4. Forward multiple services in one SSH session</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ssh -L 1234:localhost:3306 -L 8080:localhost:80 ubuntu@10.129.202.64</code></pre>
<p>If the pivot host exposes several useful localhost-only services, you can add multiple <code>-L</code> rules to the same SSH command.</p>
<h3>Dynamic port forwarding with SSH and SOCKS</h3>
<p>The second case is more general. You already control the Ubuntu pivot host, but you do not yet know what exists on the internal network behind it. Instead of forwarding a single service, you build a SOCKS tunnel and push your enumeration traffic through it.</p>
<p><strong>1. Confirm that the host is truly dual-homed</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">ubuntu@WEB01:~$ ifconfig

ens192: inet 10.129.202.64
ens224: inet 172.16.5.129
lo:     inet 127.0.0.1</code></pre>
<p>The key detail is that the pivot host has one interface in the reachable network and another in the internal network:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>ens192</code> is on the <code>10.129.202.0/16</code> side, which the attacker can already reach.</li>
<li><code>ens224</code> is on <code>172.16.5.0/23</code>, which the attacker cannot reach directly.</li>
</ul>
<p>That makes this host a valid pivot point.</p>
<img alt="Pasted image 20250529220839" src="assets/posts/pivoting-tunneling-port-forwarding/Pasted image 20250529220839.png"/>
<p><strong>2. Start SSH dynamic port forwarding locally</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">ssh -D 9050 ubuntu@10.129.202.64</code></pre>
<p><code>-D 9050</code> creates a local SOCKS listener. Any TCP traffic sent to <code>127.0.0.1:9050</code> will enter the SSH tunnel first and then be routed onward by the pivot host.</p>
<p><strong>3. Point proxychains at that SOCKS listener</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ tail -4 /etc/proxychains.conf

# meanwile
# defaults set to "tor"
socks4 127.0.0.1 9050</code></pre>
<p>After this change, any TCP tool launched through <code>proxychains</code> will send its traffic to local port <code>9050</code> first.</p>
<p><strong>4. Test the tunnel with controlled internal scanning</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ proxychains nmap -v -sn 172.16.5.1-200

ProxyChains-3.1 (http://proxychains.sf.net)
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.5:80-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-OK
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.6:80-&lt;--timeout</code></pre>
<p>The goal here is not a perfect map of the whole subnet. It is a quick confirmation that packets are actually crossing the tunnel and reaching internal systems.</p>
<p><strong>5. Enumerate a specific internal target with a TCP connect scan</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-shell-session">Chenduoduo@htb[/htb]$ proxychains nmap -v -Pn -sT 172.16.5.19

ProxyChains-3.1 (http://proxychains.sf.net)
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.19:445-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-OK
Discovered open port 445/tcp on 172.16.5.19
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.19:135-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-OK
Discovered open port 135/tcp on 172.16.5.19
|S-chain|-&lt;&gt;-127.0.0.1:9050-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-172.16.5.19:3389-&lt;&gt;&lt;&gt;-OK
Discovered open port 3389/tcp on 172.16.5.19</code></pre>
<p>Two operational details matter here:</p>
<ul>
<li>When scanning through <code>proxychains</code>, prefer a full TCP connect scan with <code>-sT</code> instead of a SYN scan.</li>
<li>Many Windows systems will not answer ICMP, so scanning a known host with <code>-Pn</code> is often more reliable.</li>
</ul>
<p>At this point we know that <code>172.16.5.19</code> exposes <code>445</code>, <code>135</code>, and <code>3389</code>. That means the tunnel is not just alive; it is already useful for RDP, Metasploit auxiliary enumeration, SMB interaction, and other next-step actions.</p>
<p><strong>Using xfreerdp through proxychains</strong></p>
<pre><code class="language-bash">proxychains xfreerdp /v:172.16.5.19 /u:victor /p:pass@123</code></pre>
<p>If the credentials are valid, this pushes RDP through the same SOCKS tunnel. From that point on, later steps such as Metasploit, RDP, and SMB are all happening by pivoting through the Ubuntu host.</p>
<h3>Using Metasploit with Proxychains</h3>
`;

